/* Microbiology 4: Gram-negative bacteria */
Z("micro4",0,"m",["Neisseria meningitidis and N. gonorrhoeae are Gram-negative kidney-shaped diplococci; meningococcus causes meningitis and septicaemia, gonococcus causes gonorrhoea.","Two cousins in pairs like coffee beans: the meningococcus travels through blood to the brain wrapped in a capsule; the gonococcus lives on genital surfaces using pili to cling.",["Both: oxidase +, catalase +, grow on chocolate agar/Thayer–Martin in 5–10% CO2.","Meningococcus: capsule (A, B, C, W, Y, X), ferments glucose and maltose; droplet spread; endotoxin → purpura fulminans, Waterhouse–Friderichsen.","Gonococcus: no capsule, ferments glucose only; pili, Opa, IgA protease, antigenic variation; urethritis, PID, ophthalmia neonatorum.","Lab: Gram stain of CSF/urethral pus (intracellular diplococci), culture, sugar fermentation, antigen tests, NAAT."],"Meningococcus = Maltose + glucose; Gonococcus = Glucose only."],
"Neisseria are Gram-negative, non-motile, non-sporing, oxidase-positive, kidney-shaped diplococci with adjacent sides concave. They are fastidious and grow best on enriched media (chocolate agar, Mueller–Hinton) and selective media (Thayer–Martin, modified New York City) at 35–37 °C in 5–10% CO2. Neisseria meningitidis is capsulated (serogroups A, B, C, W-135, Y, X based on capsular polysaccharide); it colonises the nasopharynx of carriers and spreads by droplets. Virulence: capsule (antiphagocytic), pili, outer membrane proteins, lipooligosaccharide (endotoxin, abundant blebs), IgA1 protease, iron acquisition. Diseases: meningitis (epidemic in sub-Saharan 'meningitis belt'), meningococcaemia with petechial/purpuric rash, purpura fulminans, DIC, Waterhouse–Friderichsen syndrome (adrenal haemorrhage), chronic meningococcaemia; risk in complement (C5–C9) deficiency and asplenia. Neisseria gonorrhoeae is non-capsulated with pili (adhesion, antigenic and phase variation), Opa proteins, porins, LOS and IgA1 protease; transmitted sexually. Diseases: in men acute urethritis with purulent discharge, epididymitis, prostatitis; in women endocervicitis, PID (salpingitis → infertility, ectopic pregnancy), Bartholinitis; both — proctitis, pharyngitis, disseminated gonococcal infection (arthritis–dermatitis); newborns — ophthalmia neonatorum. Laboratory diagnosis: specimens — CSF, blood, petechial scraping, nasopharyngeal swab (meningococcus); urethral/endocervical discharge (gonococcus); Gram stain shows intracellular Gram-negative diplococci in pus cells; culture on chocolate/Thayer–Martin agar (transport rapidly, avoid cold); oxidase +, catalase +; carbohydrate utilisation (CTA sugars); latex agglutination for capsular antigen in CSF; PCR/NAAT (preferred for gonorrhoea); antibiotic sensitivity (penicillinase-producing N. gonorrhoeae, ceftriaxone resistance). Prevention: meningococcal polysaccharide and conjugate vaccines, chemoprophylaxis of contacts (rifampicin, ciprofloxacin); no vaccine for gonorrhoea.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","N. meningitidis","N. gonorrhoeae"],["Capsule","Present","Absent"],["Sugar fermentation","Glucose + maltose","Glucose only"],["Site","Nasopharynx","Genital mucosa"],["Transmission","Droplets","Sexual, perinatal"],["β-lactamase","Rare","Common (PPNG)"],["Vaccine","Available","None"],["Diseases","Meningitis, meningococcaemia","Gonorrhoea, PID, ophthalmia neonatorum"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Gram stain: intracellular Gram-negative diplococci","Culture: chocolate agar, Thayer–Martin (VCN — vancomycin, colistin, nystatin), 5–10% CO2","Oxidase +, catalase +","Carbohydrate utilisation","Antigen detection (CSF latex), PCR/NAAT"]],
["Virulence factors",[["Factor","Organism","Role"],["Capsule","Meningococcus","Antiphagocytic"],["Pili","Both","Adhesion, antigenic variation"],["LOS (endotoxin)","Both (meningococcus abundant)","Shock, DIC"],["IgA1 protease","Both","Mucosal survival"],["Opa, Por","Both","Adhesion, invasion"]]]],
{i:"Pathogenic Neisseria are strict human pathogens; meningococcal disease can kill within hours, while gonorrhoea is a common sexually transmitted infection with growing drug resistance.",f:"Meningococcus: nasopharynx → blood (capsule, LOS) → meningitis / purpura / DIC / adrenal haemorrhage\nGonococcus: genital mucosa (pili, Opa) → urethritis/cervicitis → PID, DGI; newborn → ophthalmia",c:"Gram stain showing intracellular diplococci with culture and sugar tests or NAAT confirms Neisseria infections; vaccination and chemoprophylaxis control meningococcal disease."});

Z("micro4",1,"m",["Meningococcus and gonococcus look alike under the microscope but differ in capsule, sugars used, site of infection and disease.","Twins wearing the same clothes (Gram-negative diplococci), but one has a coat (capsule) and drinks maltose.",["Meningococcus: capsulated, glucose + maltose fermenter, nasopharynx, meningitis.","Gonococcus: no capsule, glucose only, genital tract, gonorrhoea.","Meningococcus vaccine available; gonococcus has no vaccine.","Gonococcus often penicillinase-producing."],"M for Maltose and Meningitis."],
"Neisseria meningitidis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae are Gram-negative, oxidase-positive diplococci with similar morphology and growth requirements, but differ as follows: meningococci possess a polysaccharide capsule (serogroups A, B, C, W, Y, X), oxidise both glucose and maltose, colonise the nasopharynx, spread by respiratory droplets and cause meningitis and septicaemia; gonococci are non-capsulated, oxidise glucose only, colonise the genital mucosa, spread sexually and perinatally, and cause gonorrhoea, PID and ophthalmia neonatorum. Meningococci grow on blood agar and are less fastidious; gonococci need chocolate/Thayer–Martin agar. Penicillinase production is common in gonococci and rare in meningococci. Vaccines exist only against meningococci.",
[["Differences",[["Feature","Meningococcus","Gonococcus"],["Capsule","Present","Absent"],["Pili","Present","Present"],["Glucose","+","+"],["Maltose","+","−"],["Growth on blood agar","Good","Poor"],["Habitat","Nasopharynx","Urogenital tract"],["Transmission","Droplet","Sexual"],["Intracellular in pus","In CSF neutrophils","In urethral pus neutrophils"],["Endotoxin effects","Prominent (shock, DIC)","Less"],["Penicillinase","Rare","Common"],["Vaccine","Yes (conjugate, polysaccharide)","No"],["Chemoprophylaxis","Rifampicin, ciprofloxacin for contacts","Ocular prophylaxis in newborns"]]]],
{c:"Despite identical morphology, capsule, maltose fermentation and clinical setting readily distinguish the two pathogenic Neisseria."});

Z("micro4",2,"m",["Escherichia coli is a Gram-negative bacillus that is a normal gut resident, but some strains cause urinary infections, diarrhoea, neonatal meningitis and sepsis.","Mostly a friendly gut tenant; certain strains carry toolkits (toxins, adhesins) that turn them into specialised criminals.",["Gram-negative, motile bacilli; lactose fermenter (pink on MacConkey), indole +, MR +, VP −, citrate − (IMViC ++−−).","Antigens: O (somatic), H (flagellar), K (capsular).","Commonest cause of UTI (uropathogenic E. coli with P fimbriae), neonatal meningitis (K1), sepsis.","Diarrhoeagenic: ETEC (traveller's diarrhoea; LT, ST), EPEC (infant), EHEC O157:H7 (Shiga toxin, HUS), EIEC (dysentery), EAEC (persistent)."],"E. coli IMViC = '++ − −'; EHEC → HUS."],
"Escherichia coli is a Gram-negative, motile (peritrichous), non-sporing, facultatively anaerobic bacillus of the family Enterobacteriaceae, often fimbriated and sometimes capsulated. It grows on ordinary media; on MacConkey agar it forms pink lactose-fermenting colonies; some strains are β-haemolytic on blood agar; on EMB agar colonies show a green metallic sheen. Biochemically: catalase +, oxidase −, reduces nitrates, ferments glucose with gas, IMViC ++−− (indole +, methyl red +, Voges–Proskauer −, citrate −), urease −, H2S −. Antigens: O (somatic, LPS; > 170), H (flagellar), K (capsular — K1 associated with neonatal meningitis) and F (fimbrial). Toxins: heat-labile enterotoxin (LT; like cholera toxin, ↑ cAMP), heat-stable enterotoxin (ST; ↑ cGMP), Shiga-like toxins/verotoxins (inhibit protein synthesis; HUS), haemolysins, endotoxin. Pathogenesis and diseases: urinary tract infection (commonest cause; uropathogenic E. coli with P fimbriae, haemolysin, K antigen — cystitis, pyelonephritis); diarrhoeal disease by pathotypes — ETEC (traveller's diarrhoea, children in developing countries; LT, ST, colonisation factors), EPEC (infantile diarrhoea; attaching and effacing lesions via bundle-forming pili and intimin), EHEC/STEC O157:H7 (haemorrhagic colitis, haemolytic uraemic syndrome; Shiga toxin; sorbitol non-fermenter), EIEC (dysentery-like; invasion like Shigella), EAEC (persistent diarrhoea; stacked-brick adherence), DAEC; neonatal meningitis (K1); septicaemia, wound infections, pneumonia, intra-abdominal infections. Laboratory diagnosis: specimens (urine, stool, pus, blood, CSF); Gram stain; culture on blood and MacConkey agar, CLED for urine; biochemical identification; quantitative urine culture (≥ 10⁵ CFU/mL significant bacteriuria); serotyping, toxin detection (ELISA, PCR), sorbitol MacConkey for O157; antibiotic susceptibility (ESBL, carbapenemase production).",
[["Morphology and culture",["Gram-negative bacilli, motile, may be capsulated/fimbriated","MacConkey: pink lactose-fermenting colonies","EMB: green metallic sheen","IMViC: + + − −; oxidase −, catalase +"]],
["Diarrhoeagenic E. coli",[["Pathotype","Mechanism","Disease"],["ETEC","LT (↑cAMP), ST (↑cGMP), CFA","Traveller's diarrhoea, infant diarrhoea"],["EPEC","Attaching–effacing lesions (intimin, BFP)","Infantile diarrhoea"],["EHEC (O157:H7)","Shiga toxin","Haemorrhagic colitis, HUS"],["EIEC","Invasion (plasmid)","Dysentery"],["EAEC","Stacked-brick adherence, biofilm","Persistent diarrhoea"]]],
["Extraintestinal infections",["UTI: commonest cause (UPEC — P fimbriae, haemolysin)","Neonatal meningitis (K1 capsule)","Septicaemia, Gram-negative shock","Wound, biliary, peritoneal infections; pneumonia in hospitals"]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Urine: semi-quantitative culture; ≥ 10⁵ CFU/mL significant","Stool: culture, serotyping, toxin PCR/ELISA; SMAC agar for O157","Blood/CSF culture","Antibiotic sensitivity: ESBL and carbapenemase detection"]]],
{i:"E. coli is the commonest Gram-negative bacterium of human disease; virulence genes acquired by plasmids and phages define its pathotypes.",f:"Commensal gut E. coli + virulence genes\n ├ UPEC (P fimbriae) → cystitis, pyelonephritis\n ├ K1 → neonatal meningitis\n ├ ETEC/EPEC/EHEC/EIEC/EAEC → diarrhoea, dysentery, HUS\n └ bloodstream → sepsis, endotoxic shock",c:"E. coli ranges from harmless commensal to versatile pathogen; culture, biochemistry and pathotype-specific tests guide diagnosis, and rising ESBL and carbapenem resistance complicate treatment."});

Z("micro4",3,"m",["Shigella is a non-motile Gram-negative bacillus that invades the colon lining and causes bacillary dysentery with blood and mucus in stools; very few bacteria (10–100) can cause disease.","Shigella is a burglar that breaks into colon cells, moves from cell to cell, and leaves ulcers behind.",["Non-motile, non-lactose fermenter (except S. sonnei late), no H2S, no gas (except some S. flexneri 6).","Four species/groups: A S. dysenteriae (Shiga toxin, most severe), B S. flexneri (commonest in India), C S. boydii, D S. sonnei (late lactose fermenter, mannitol +).","Pathogenesis: invades M cells → spreads cell to cell (actin tails) → ulcers; Shiga toxin → HUS.","Lab: stool microscopy (pus cells, RBCs), culture on MacConkey, DCA, XLD; slide agglutination serotyping."],"Shigella groups 'A Dysenteriae, B Flexneri, C Boydii, D Sonnei'."],
"Shigella are Gram-negative, non-motile, non-capsulated, non-sporing bacilli of Enterobacteriaceae, causing bacillary dysentery (shigellosis). Culture: on MacConkey and deoxycholate citrate agar (DCA) they form pale non-lactose-fermenting colonies (S. sonnei is a late lactose fermenter); on XLD agar red colonies without black centres; enrichment in selenite F broth. Biochemistry: catalase +, oxidase −, ferment glucose without gas (except some S. flexneri 6 and S. boydii), H2S −, urease −, citrate −, non-motile. Classification (based on mannitol fermentation and antigens): group A — S. dysenteriae (mannitol −; type 1 produces Shiga toxin), group B — S. flexneri (commonest in India), group C — S. boydii, group D — S. sonnei (commonest in developed countries; late lactose fermenter, ornithine decarboxylase +). Pathogenesis: faeco-oral, very low infective dose (10–100 bacilli); bacteria invade through M cells, are taken up by macrophages, induce apoptosis and IL-1 release, then invade enterocytes from the basolateral side, multiply, spread laterally to adjacent cells via actin polymerisation (IcsA), causing inflammation, microabscesses and superficial ulcers of the colonic mucosa; invasion genes are on a large virulence plasmid. Toxins: Shiga toxin (S. dysenteriae type 1) — A–B toxin inhibiting 60S ribosome (28S rRNA), causing enterotoxic, cytotoxic and neurotoxic effects and haemolytic uraemic syndrome; enterotoxins ShET-1, ShET-2; endotoxin. Clinical: fever, abdominal cramps, tenesmus, frequent small stools with blood and mucus; complications — dehydration, HUS, reactive arthritis, toxic megacolon. Laboratory diagnosis: fresh stool (mucus flecks) or rectal swab, transported in buffered glycerol saline; microscopy — numerous pus cells, RBCs, macrophages; culture on MacConkey, DCA, XLD with enrichment; biochemical identification and slide agglutination with group and type-specific antisera; antibiotic susceptibility (high resistance; ciprofloxacin, azithromycin, ceftriaxone).",
[["Classification",[["Group","Species","Features"],["A","S. dysenteriae","Mannitol −; Shiga toxin (type 1); severe, HUS"],["B","S. flexneri","Mannitol +; commonest in India"],["C","S. boydii","Mannitol +"],["D","S. sonnei","Late lactose fermenter; ornithine decarboxylase +; mild"]]],
["Pathogenesis",["Low infective dose (10–100)","M cell uptake → macrophage apoptosis → IL-1 → inflammation","Basolateral invasion of colonocytes; actin-based cell-to-cell spread","Mucosal ulcers, microabscesses","Shiga toxin → HUS, CNS effects"]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Stool: pus cells, RBCs, macrophages","Culture: MacConkey, DCA, XLD; selenite F enrichment","Biochemistry: non-motile, NLF, no H2S, no gas","Serotyping by slide agglutination","Antibiotic sensitivity"]],
["Bacillary vs amoebic dysentery",[["Feature","Bacillary","Amoebic"],["Stool","Small, frequent, blood + mucus, no odour","Large, offensive, blood mixed with faeces"],["Cells","Many pus cells, macrophages","Few pus cells, trophozoites with RBCs"],["Charcot–Leyden crystals","Absent","Present"],["Onset","Acute, fever","Insidious, afebrile"]]]],
{i:"Shigellosis is a major cause of bloody diarrhoea in children in developing countries; S. dysenteriae type 1 causes the most severe epidemics.",f:"Ingestion (10–100 bacilli) → colon M cells → macrophage death\n → basolateral invasion of colonocytes → cell-to-cell spread\n → inflammation, ulcers → dysentery\n S. dysenteriae 1 → Shiga toxin → HUS",c:"Shigella's invasive mechanism explains dysentery with a very low infective dose; stool culture with serotyping and susceptibility testing guide treatment amid widespread resistance."});

Z("micro4",4,"m",["Salmonella is a motile Gram-negative bacillus; S. Typhi and Paratyphi cause enteric (typhoid) fever, while other serotypes cause food poisoning (gastroenteritis).","Typhoid salmonella is a stealth invader that hides inside macrophages and spreads through the body; food-poisoning salmonella just irritates the gut.",["Motile, non-lactose fermenter, H2S positive (except Paratyphi A), citrate variable.","Antigens: O (somatic), H (flagellar, phase 1 & 2), Vi (capsular — S. Typhi). Kauffmann–White scheme.","Typhoid pathogenesis: ingestion → Peyer's patches → macrophages → bacteraemia → reticuloendothelial organs → secondary bacteraemia; carriers in gall bladder.","Lab: blood culture (week 1), Widal (week 2), stool & urine culture (week 3), bone marrow culture (best)."],"Typhoid tests by week 'BASU': Blood, Antibodies (Widal), Stool, Urine."],
"Salmonella are Gram-negative, motile (peritrichous; except S. Gallinarum–Pullorum), non-sporing, non-capsulated (except Vi of S. Typhi) bacilli of Enterobacteriaceae. Culture: on MacConkey and DCA pale non-lactose-fermenting colonies; on DCA and XLD, colonies with black centres (H2S); Wilson–Blair bismuth sulphite medium — jet-black colonies with metallic sheen (S. Typhi); enrichment in selenite F or tetrathionate broth. Biochemistry: ferment glucose (S. Typhi without gas; others with gas), H2S + (S. Paratyphi A negative), indole −, urease −, MR +, VP −, citrate + (S. Typhi and Paratyphi A negative). Antigenic structure: O (somatic, LPS; groups A–Z), H (flagellar; phase 1 specific and phase 2 non-specific), Vi (surface polysaccharide envelope of S. Typhi, inhibits O agglutination, antiphagocytic); Kauffmann–White classification groups > 2500 serotypes. Pathogenesis of enteric fever: ingestion of contaminated food/water (infective dose ~10⁵); bacteria survive gastric acid, invade M cells of Peyer's patches in the ileum, are taken up by macrophages where they survive and multiply, spread to mesenteric lymph nodes and bloodstream (primary bacteraemia), seed liver, spleen, bone marrow and gall bladder, multiply and cause secondary bacteraemia with onset of fever (incubation 10–14 days); bile re-infects the intestine causing Peyer's patch hyperplasia, necrosis and ulcers (perforation and haemorrhage in third week). Clinical: step-ladder fever, headache, relative bradycardia, rose spots, splenomegaly, coated tongue, constipation or diarrhoea. Carriers (convalescent, chronic — gall bladder) are important reservoirs (Typhoid Mary). Non-typhoidal salmonellae (S. Typhimurium, S. Enteritidis) cause gastroenteritis (food poisoning) and bacteraemia in immunocompromised and sickle cell (osteomyelitis). Laboratory diagnosis of enteric fever: blood culture (positive in 90% in first week; bile broth), bone marrow culture (most sensitive, even after antibiotics), clot culture, stool and urine culture (from second–third week; for carriers), Widal test (from second week; rising titres), rapid tests (Typhidot IgM), PCR; carriers detected by Vi antibody and repeated stool/bile cultures. Treatment: ceftriaxone, azithromycin (resistance to fluoroquinolones). Prevention: safe water, sanitation, Vi polysaccharide and typhoid conjugate vaccines, oral Ty21a.",
[["Antigenic structure",[["Antigen","Nature","Significance"],["O","Somatic LPS, heat stable","Serogroups; Widal O agglutinins (recent infection)"],["H","Flagellar protein, heat labile","Serotypes; Widal H agglutinins (persist, vaccination)"],["Vi","Capsular polysaccharide","Virulence, carrier detection, Vi vaccine"]]],
["Pathogenesis of typhoid",["Ingestion → ileum → M cells of Peyer's patches","Survival in macrophages → mesenteric nodes → primary bacteraemia","Multiplication in RE system (liver, spleen, marrow) → secondary bacteraemia (fever)","Gall bladder → bile → re-infection of gut → ulceration of Peyer's patches","Complications: perforation, haemorrhage, myocarditis; carrier state"]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",[["Week","Test","Yield"],["1st","Blood culture","90%"],["2nd","Widal test","Rising titres"],["3rd","Stool/urine culture","Positive"],["Any","Bone marrow culture","Most sensitive"]]]],
{i:"Enteric fever remains endemic in India; increasing antimicrobial resistance (MDR, XDR strains) makes laboratory confirmation and susceptibility testing important.",x:[["Enteric fever vs non-typhoidal salmonellosis",[["Feature","Enteric fever","Gastroenteritis"],["Serotypes","Typhi, Paratyphi A, B, C","Typhimurium, Enteritidis"],["Host","Humans only","Animals, poultry"],["Incubation","10–14 days","6–48 h"],["Disease","Systemic","Local enteritis"],["Blood culture","Positive","Usually negative"]]]],f:"Contaminated food/water → Peyer's patches (M cells)\n → macrophages → mesenteric nodes → primary bacteraemia\n → RE organs → secondary bacteraemia (fever)\n → gall bladder → bile → ileal ulcers (perforation 3rd week) / carrier",c:"Salmonella Typhi is an intracellular pathogen causing systemic enteric fever; diagnosis depends on choosing the right specimen for the week of illness, with blood culture as the gold standard."});

Z("micro4",5,"m",["The Widal test detects antibodies (agglutinins) against Salmonella Typhi and Paratyphi O and H antigens in the patient's serum to support diagnosis of enteric fever.","Like checking the guest book to see whether the body has 'met' typhoid bacteria — a rising number of signatures means a recent visit.",["Tube agglutination with TO, TH, AH, BH antigens; titres measured.","O agglutinins appear earlier and fall sooner (recent infection); H persist longer (also after vaccination).","Significant: rising titre (4-fold) in paired sera; single TO ≥ 1:160 and TH ≥ 1:160 in endemic areas.","False positives: previous vaccination, anamnestic reaction, other infections; false negatives: early disease, antibiotics, carriers."],"Widal: 'O = Onset (early), H = Hangs on (late)'."],
"The Widal test is a tube agglutination test for antibodies to Salmonella Typhi and Paratyphi in patient serum. Antigens: TO (S. Typhi O, somatic), TH (S. Typhi H, flagellar), AH and BH (Paratyphi A and B H antigens). Doubling dilutions of serum (1:20 to 1:640) are mixed with standard antigen suspensions and incubated at 37 °C (H) or 50–55 °C (O) for 4 and 24 hours. H agglutination produces large loose fluffy clumps; O agglutination produces compact granular deposits. Antibodies appear by the end of the first week, rise during the second and third weeks. Interpretation: a four-fold rise in titre between paired sera 7–10 days apart is diagnostic; a single high titre (O ≥ 1:160 and H ≥ 1:160 in India) is suggestive; baseline titres in the local population should be considered. Limitations: false positives — prior TAB vaccination (H high), anamnestic reaction in other fevers, cross-reacting infections (malaria, dengue), chronic liver disease; false negatives — early sampling, prior antibiotics, immunosuppression, prozone; carriers may have Vi antibodies only. Rapid alternatives: Typhidot (IgM), Tubex, slide Widal.",
[["Interpretation",[["Finding","Interpretation"],["4-fold rise in paired sera","Diagnostic of active infection"],["High O alone","Recent infection"],["High H alone","Past infection, vaccination, anamnestic"],["Vi antibodies","Chronic carrier"]]],
["Limitations",["Low sensitivity in first week","Cross-reactions with other Enterobacteriaceae","Prior vaccination or infection raises baseline","Antibiotics blunt response"]]],
{c:"Widal test is a useful but imperfect adjunct; blood culture remains the gold standard for enteric fever diagnosis."});

Z("micro4",6,"m",["Vibrio cholerae is a comma-shaped, highly motile Gram-negative bacillus that causes cholera — profuse 'rice-water' diarrhoea — by a toxin that makes the gut pump out water.","Cholera toxin jams the gut's 'open tap' switch (cAMP), so salt and water pour out continuously.",["Curved rods, single polar flagellum — 'darting' (shooting star) motility; oxidase +.","Grows in alkaline media: alkaline peptone water (enrichment), TCBS (yellow colonies); VR transport medium.","Cholera toxin (A–B): A1 ADP-ribosylates Gs → ↑ adenylate cyclase → ↑ cAMP → Cl⁻ and water secretion.","Serogroups O1 (classical, El Tor) and O139 cause epidemics; rice-water stools; ORS is life-saving."],"Cholera = 'Comma, Darting, TCBS yellow, cAMP up, ORS'."],
"Vibrio cholerae is a Gram-negative, curved (comma-shaped) bacillus, actively motile by a single polar flagellum (darting/shooting-star motility on hanging drop), non-sporing, non-capsulated (O139 has a capsule). It is aerobic, oxidase-positive, grows at alkaline pH (8.2–9.6), salt-tolerant but not halophilic. Media: transport — Venkatraman–Ramakrishnan medium, Cary–Blair; enrichment — alkaline peptone water; selective — TCBS (thiosulphate citrate bile salt sucrose agar: yellow sucrose-fermenting colonies), bile salt agar, MacConkey (non-lactose fermenting initially). Biochemistry: catalase +, oxidase +, indole +, cholera red reaction, string test +. Classification by O antigen: > 200 serogroups; O1 (biotypes classical and El Tor; serotypes Ogawa, Inaba, Hikojima) and O139 (Bengal) cause epidemic cholera; non-O1/non-O139 cause sporadic diarrhoea. Pathogenesis: faeco-oral via contaminated water or food; large infective dose (10⁸; less with achlorhydria); bacteria pass the stomach, use flagella and mucinase to penetrate mucus, adhere to the small intestinal mucosa by toxin-coregulated pili (TCP), multiply and secrete cholera toxin (encoded by CTXφ phage). Cholera toxin: one A subunit and five B subunits; B binds GM1 ganglioside; A1 ADP-ribosylates Gsα, locking adenylate cyclase on; raised cAMP causes active secretion of Cl⁻ (CFTR) and inhibits Na⁺ absorption → massive isotonic fluid loss. No invasion or inflammation. Clinical: sudden painless profuse watery 'rice-water' stools (up to 1 L/hour) with fishy odour, vomiting, rapid dehydration, hypovolaemic shock, metabolic acidosis, hypokalaemia; mortality > 50% untreated, < 1% with rehydration. Laboratory diagnosis: rice-water stool collected before antibiotics; hanging drop (darting motility) and immobilisation with specific antiserum; Gram stain; culture on TCBS after APW enrichment; oxidase, biochemical tests, slide agglutination with O1 and O139 antisera; rapid dipstick tests; PCR. Treatment: ORS/IV Ringer lactate; doxycycline or azithromycin shortens illness. Prevention: safe water, sanitation, oral cholera vaccines.",
[["Laboratory diagnosis",["Specimen: rice-water stool (VR medium/Cary–Blair for transport)","Hanging drop: darting motility, immobilised by antiserum","Gram stain: Gram-negative curved bacilli ('fish in stream')","Enrichment: alkaline peptone water 6–8 h","Culture: TCBS — yellow colonies","Oxidase +, string test +, cholera red +","Slide agglutination with O1, O139 antisera; biotyping"]],
["Cholera toxin mechanism",["B subunits bind GM1 ganglioside","A1 → ADP-ribosylation of Gsα","Adenylate cyclase permanently active → ↑ cAMP","↑ Cl⁻ secretion, ↓ NaCl absorption → watery diarrhoea"]],
["Other virulence factors",["Toxin-coregulated pilus (TCP) — colonisation","Mucinase, motility","Accessory cholera enterotoxin, zonula occludens toxin","Neuraminidase"]]],
{i:"Cholera is the classic toxin-mediated secretory diarrhoea; seven pandemics have occurred, the current one by El Tor biotype.",f:"Contaminated water → small intestine → TCP adhesion\n → cholera toxin (CTXφ) → GM1 binding → A1 → Gs → ↑ cAMP\n → Cl⁻ & water secretion → rice-water stools → dehydration, shock",c:"Cholera kills by dehydration, not invasion; rapid diagnosis with TCBS culture and serotyping supports outbreak control, while ORS remains the life-saving treatment."});

Z("micro4",7,"m",["Classical and El Tor are the two biotypes of Vibrio cholerae O1; El Tor causes the current (7th) pandemic and survives better in the environment, causing more mild and carrier cases.","El Tor is the more cunning cousin: hardier, spreads silently through mild cases and carriers.",["Classical: polymyxin B sensitive, classical phage IV sensitive, VP −, no haemolysis, no chick RBC agglutination.","El Tor: polymyxin B resistant, El Tor phage 5 sensitive, VP +, haemolysis of sheep RBC (variable), agglutinates chick RBC.","El Tor: more asymptomatic infections and carriers, survives longer in environment.","Both: same toxin mechanism and treatment."],"El Tor = 'Tough': resistant to polymyxin, VP +, agglutinates chick RBC."],
"Vibrio cholerae O1 has two biotypes, classical and El Tor, which differ in phenotypic tests and epidemiology. The classical biotype caused the first six cholera pandemics; El Tor (first isolated at El Tor quarantine camp, Egypt) caused the seventh pandemic from 1961. Classical strains are sensitive to polymyxin B (50 IU disc), lysed by classical phage IV, VP negative, non-haemolytic, do not agglutinate chicken red cells. El Tor strains are resistant to polymyxin B, lysed by El Tor phage 5, VP positive, haemolyse sheep RBCs (Greig test, variable), agglutinate chick erythrocytes. Epidemiologically, El Tor produces a higher ratio of inapparent to apparent infections (about 30–100:1 vs 5–10:1 for classical), longer carrier state, better survival in water and environment, and hence wider spread. Hybrid and variant El Tor strains producing classical-type toxin are now common.",
[["Differences",[["Test/feature","Classical","El Tor"],["Polymyxin B (50 IU)","Sensitive","Resistant"],["Classical phage IV","Lysed","Not lysed"],["El Tor phage 5","Not lysed","Lysed"],["VP reaction","Negative","Positive"],["Haemolysis of sheep RBC","Negative","Positive (variable)"],["Chick RBC agglutination","Negative","Positive"],["Inapparent:apparent infections","~5–10:1","~30–100:1"],["Carrier state","Short","Longer"],["Environmental survival","Poor","Good"],["Pandemics","1st–6th","7th (current)"]]]],
{c:"Biotyping helps epidemiological tracking; El Tor's hardiness and silent spread explain its pandemic success."});

Z("micro4",8,"m",["Helicobacter pylori is a spiral, highly motile Gram-negative bacterium that lives in the stomach and causes gastritis, peptic ulcers, gastric cancer and MALT lymphoma.","H. pylori carries its own antacid (urease → ammonia) to survive in the stomach's acid sea.",["Curved/spiral, multiple sheathed polar flagella; microaerophilic; urease, oxidase, catalase +.","Urease makes ammonia → neutralises acid; also damages mucosa.","Virulence: CagA, VacA, adhesins (BabA).","Diagnosis: urea breath test, stool antigen, rapid urease test and histology on biopsy, culture, serology. Treatment: triple therapy (PPI + clarithromycin + amoxicillin)."],"H. pylori 'Urease → Ammonia → survives Acid'."],
"Helicobacter pylori is a Gram-negative, curved or spiral bacillus with 4–6 sheathed unipolar flagella (corkscrew motility), microaerophilic, growing slowly (3–7 days) on enriched media (chocolate, Skirrow's) at 37 °C; strongly urease-positive, oxidase and catalase positive. It colonises the gastric mucus layer of the antrum, adhering to epithelium; infection is acquired in childhood (faeco-oral, oral–oral) and persists for life without treatment. Virulence factors: urease (ammonia neutralises acid, toxic to epithelium), flagella, adhesins (BabA, SabA), vacuolating cytotoxin VacA, cytotoxin-associated gene A (CagA, injected by type IV secretion system — inflammation, oncogenic), LPS, catalase. Diseases: chronic active gastritis (antral), peptic ulcer (duodenal and gastric), gastric adenocarcinoma (class I carcinogen) and gastric MALT lymphoma; associated with iron deficiency and ITP. Diagnosis: invasive (endoscopic biopsy) — rapid urease test (CLO test; colour change pink), histology (Giemsa, Warthin–Starry), culture, PCR; non-invasive — urea breath test (¹³C or ¹⁴C labelled urea; best for diagnosis and confirming eradication), stool antigen test, serology (IgG; cannot distinguish current from past). Treatment: PPI-based triple therapy (PPI + clarithromycin + amoxicillin/metronidazole for 14 days) or bismuth quadruple therapy.",
[["Diagnostic tests",[["Test","Type","Comment"],["Rapid urease test","Invasive","Quick, cheap"],["Histology","Invasive","Shows gastritis too"],["Culture","Invasive","Antibiotic sensitivity"],["Urea breath test","Non-invasive","Best for eradication check"],["Stool antigen","Non-invasive","Diagnosis and follow-up"],["Serology","Non-invasive","Cannot confirm cure"]]]],
{c:"H. pylori is the main cause of peptic ulcer and a major gastric carcinogen; eradication cures ulcers and reduces cancer risk."});

Z("micro4",9,"m",["Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a hardy, motile Gram-negative bacillus that causes hospital infections — burns, wounds, ventilator pneumonia, UTIs — and is notoriously drug-resistant.","A survivor that thrives in moist hospital places (sinks, ventilators) and paints its colonies green with pigments.",["Gram-negative bacilli, motile (single polar flagellum), strict aerobe, oxidase +, non-lactose fermenter.","Pigments: pyocyanin (blue-green), pyoverdin (fluorescent yellow-green), pyorubin, pyomelanin; grape-like/fruity odour.","Virulence: exotoxin A (ADP-ribosylates EF-2), elastase, alginate (mucoid in cystic fibrosis), biofilm, type III secretion.","Infections: burns, wounds, VAP, UTI, otitis externa (swimmer's ear, malignant otitis), keratitis, cystic fibrosis lung, ecthyma gangrenosum; drugs — piperacillin–tazobactam, ceftazidime, carbapenems, aminoglycosides."],"Pseudomonas = 'Pigment green, grape smell, oxidase +, opportunist'."],
"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a Gram-negative, motile (single polar flagellum), non-sporing, strictly aerobic bacillus found widely in soil, water and moist hospital environments. It grows on simple media over a wide temperature range (including 42 °C), producing large, flat, spreading colonies with a metallic sheen, fruity (grape-like) odour and diffusible pigments: pyocyanin (blue-green, chloroform-soluble, unique to P. aeruginosa), pyoverdin/fluorescein (yellow-green fluorescent), pyorubin (red) and pyomelanin (brown-black); β-haemolysis on blood agar; non-lactose fermenter on MacConkey. Biochemistry: oxidase +, catalase +, oxidative utilisation of glucose, arginine dihydrolase +, nitrate reduction. Selective medium: cetrimide agar. Virulence factors: pili and adhesins, capsule/alginate slime (mucoid strains in cystic fibrosis), biofilm formation, LPS, exotoxin A (ADP-ribosylates EF-2 like diphtheria toxin), exoenzymes S, T, U (type III secretion), elastase, alkaline protease, phospholipase C, pyocyanin (ROS generation), and intrinsic and acquired multidrug resistance (low outer membrane permeability, efflux pumps, β-lactamases including AmpC, MBLs). Infections (opportunistic, mostly hospital-acquired): burn and wound infections (green pus), ventilator-associated pneumonia, chronic lung infection in cystic fibrosis, catheter-associated UTI, bacteraemia with ecthyma gangrenosum in neutropenia, otitis externa (swimmer's ear) and malignant otitis externa in diabetics, keratitis with contact lenses, osteomyelitis after foot puncture, hot-tub folliculitis, endocarditis in IV drug users. Laboratory diagnosis: culture with pigment, oxidase positivity, biochemical tests, antibiotic susceptibility. Treatment: anti-pseudomonal β-lactams (piperacillin–tazobactam, ceftazidime, cefepime, carbapenems), aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, colistin for resistant strains.",
[["Identification",["Gram-negative bacilli, motile","Non-lactose fermenter; oxidase +","Pigments: pyocyanin, pyoverdin; grape-like odour","Grows at 42 °C; cetrimide agar","Metallic sheen colonies, β-haemolysis"]],
["Infections",[["Site","Infection"],["Skin","Burn wounds, ecthyma gangrenosum, hot-tub folliculitis"],["Lung","VAP, cystic fibrosis"],["Ear","Swimmer's ear, malignant otitis externa (diabetics)"],["Eye","Keratitis (contact lens)"],["Urinary","Catheter-associated UTI"],["Blood","Bacteraemia in neutropenia"],["Bone","Osteomyelitis after nail puncture"]]]],
{c:"P. aeruginosa is a leading hospital pathogen; pigment and oxidase reaction aid identification, and susceptibility testing is essential given its resistance."});

Z("micro4",10,"m",["Haemophilus influenzae is a small Gram-negative coccobacillus that needs two blood factors — X (haemin) and V (NAD) — to grow; it causes meningitis, epiglottitis and pneumonia, especially type b in children.","H. influenzae is a picky eater needing two blood 'vitamins'; it can grow next to a staph colony that releases V factor — satellitism.",["Requires X factor (haemin) and V factor (NAD); grows on chocolate agar.","Satellitism: tiny colonies near S. aureus on blood agar (S. aureus supplies V factor and lyses RBCs releasing X).","Capsular types a–f; type b (PRP capsule) most virulent.","Diseases: Hib meningitis, epiglottitis, pneumonia, otitis, sinusitis, cellulitis; Hib conjugate vaccine in pentavalent."],"Hib needs 'X and V' — 'ten (X) and five (V)'."],
"Haemophilus influenzae is a small, Gram-negative, pleomorphic coccobacillus, non-motile, non-sporing; type b and other typeable strains have polysaccharide capsules (types a–f; type b — polyribosyl ribitol phosphate). It is fastidious, requiring both X factor (haemin, heat-stable, needed for cytochromes) and V factor (NAD/NADP, heat-labile) for growth. It grows on chocolate agar (heated blood releases both factors) and Levinthal or Fildes agar but poorly on blood agar (V factor is inactivated by RBC NADase). Satellitism: on blood agar, H. influenzae colonies grow larger near streaks of S. aureus, which secretes V factor and lyses RBCs. Factor requirement test using X, V and XV discs on nutrient agar identifies species (H. influenzae needs both, H. parainfluenzae only V, H. ducreyi only X). Pathogenesis: colonises upper respiratory tract; capsule (antiphagocytic), IgA protease, LOS, pili. Diseases: invasive (mainly Hib in children < 5 years) — meningitis, epiglottitis (acute airway obstruction), pneumonia, septic arthritis, cellulitis, bacteraemia; non-invasive (non-typeable strains) — otitis media, sinusitis, conjunctivitis, exacerbations of COPD. Laboratory diagnosis: CSF, blood, sputum; Gram stain; culture on chocolate agar in CO2; satellitism; X and V requirement; capsular typing (latex agglutination, Quellung); PCR. Treatment: ceftriaxone; ampicillin resistance via β-lactamase. Prevention: Hib conjugate vaccine (part of pentavalent in UIP), rifampicin prophylaxis for contacts.",
[["Factor requirements",[["Species","X factor","V factor"],["H. influenzae","+","+"],["H. parainfluenzae","−","+"],["H. ducreyi","+","−"],["H. aegyptius","+","+"]]],
["Satellitism",["Blood agar streaked with H. influenzae and S. aureus","Larger colonies near S. aureus streak","S. aureus provides V factor (NAD) and haemolysis releases X factor"]],
["Diseases",["Invasive (Hib): meningitis, epiglottitis, pneumonia, septic arthritis, cellulitis","Non-typeable: otitis media, sinusitis, conjunctivitis, COPD exacerbations"]]],
{c:"Dependence on X and V factors and satellitism identify H. influenzae; Hib conjugate vaccination has dramatically reduced invasive disease in children."});

Z("micro4",11,"m",["Bordetella pertussis is a small Gram-negative coccobacillus that causes whooping cough — bouts of violent coughing followed by a 'whoop' — mainly in unvaccinated infants.","The toxin paralyses the airway's cleaning brushes (cilia) and confuses immune signals, leaving sticky mucus that triggers paroxysms of cough.",["Small, capsulated Gram-negative coccobacillus; strict aerobe; grows on Bordet–Gengou (potato-blood-glycerol) or Regan–Lowe (charcoal) agar — 'bisected pearl' / mercury drop colonies.","Virulence: pertussis toxin (ADP-ribosylates Gi → ↑ cAMP, lymphocytosis), filamentous haemagglutinin, pertactin, tracheal cytotoxin, adenylate cyclase toxin.","Stages: catarrhal (most infectious), paroxysmal (whoop, vomiting), convalescent.","Lab: pernasal (nasopharyngeal) swab culture, PCR; marked lymphocytosis. Prevention: DPT/pentavalent (whole cell or acellular)."],"Pertussis 'Catarrhal → Paroxysmal → Convalescent' and lymphocytosis."],
"Bordetella pertussis is a small, ovoid, Gram-negative coccobacillus, non-motile, capsulated (virulent phase I), strictly aerobic and fastidious. It grows on Bordet–Gengou glycerine–potato–blood agar (with penicillin) and Regan–Lowe charcoal blood agar in 3–5 days, forming small, shiny, dome-shaped 'bisected pearl' or 'mercury drop' colonies with a narrow zone of haemolysis. Transmission is by droplets; it colonises ciliated epithelium of the respiratory tract without invading. Virulence factors: filamentous haemagglutinin and pertactin (adhesion), fimbriae, pertussis toxin (A–B toxin; ADP-ribosylates Gi protein, raising cAMP; causes lymphocytosis-promoting effect, histamine sensitisation, insulin secretion), adenylate cyclase toxin, tracheal cytotoxin (ciliostasis, epithelial damage), dermonecrotic toxin, endotoxin. Clinical whooping cough: incubation 7–14 days; catarrhal stage (1–2 weeks; rhinorrhoea, mild cough; most infectious), paroxysmal stage (2–6 weeks; repeated violent coughs ending in a high-pitched inspiratory whoop, vomiting, cyanosis), convalescent stage (weeks–months). Complications: pneumonia, convulsions, encephalopathy, subconjunctival haemorrhage, hernias, apnoea in infants. Laboratory diagnosis: pernasal or nasopharyngeal swab (cough plate method historically) cultured on Bordet–Gengou/Regan–Lowe; immunofluorescence; PCR (most sensitive); serology; marked leucocytosis with lymphocytosis. Treatment: macrolides (azithromycin). Prevention: whole-cell or acellular pertussis vaccine in DPT/pentavalent, maternal Tdap.",
[["Virulence factors",[["Factor","Action"],["Pertussis toxin","ADP-ribosylates Gi → ↑ cAMP; lymphocytosis, histamine sensitivity"],["Filamentous haemagglutinin","Adhesion to cilia"],["Pertactin","Adhesion"],["Adenylate cyclase toxin","Impairs phagocytes"],["Tracheal cytotoxin","Kills ciliated cells"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Pernasal / nasopharyngeal swab","Culture: Bordet–Gengou, Regan–Lowe charcoal agar — bisected pearl colonies","PCR (preferred)","DFA, serology","Blood: leucocytosis with lymphocytosis"]]],
{c:"Pertussis is a vaccine-preventable toxin-mediated respiratory disease that is most dangerous in young infants; PCR and culture confirm diagnosis."});

Z("micro4",12,"m",["Brucella are small Gram-negative coccobacilli that cause brucellosis (undulant/Malta fever), an occupational zoonosis from animals and unpasteurised milk.","Brucella hides inside macrophages like an enemy agent, producing waves of fever that come and go.",["Species: B. melitensis (goats, sheep — most virulent), B. abortus (cattle), B. suis (pigs), B. canis (dogs).","Transmission: unpasteurised milk/cheese, contact with infected animals, aerosols (lab acquired); veterinarians, farmers, butchers.","Undulant fever, sweats, arthralgia, sacroiliitis, hepatosplenomegaly, orchitis; granulomas.","Lab: blood/marrow culture (Castaneda biphasic medium, prolonged), standard agglutination test (≥ 1:160), Coombs test for blocking antibodies, 2-ME test, ELISA."],"Brucella = 'Milk, Malta fever, Macrophage hide-out'."],
"Brucella are small, Gram-negative, non-motile, non-capsulated, non-sporing coccobacilli; facultative intracellular pathogens. Species pathogenic to humans: B. melitensis (goats, sheep; most virulent, commonest in India), B. abortus (cattle), B. suis (pigs) and B. canis (dogs). They are slow-growing; culture on serum dextrose agar, Brucella agar, or biphasic Castaneda medium (blood culture) incubated up to 4–6 weeks; B. abortus requires 5–10% CO2. Differentiation by CO2 requirement, H2S production, urease and dye (thionin, basic fuchsin) sensitivity. Transmission: ingestion of raw milk and dairy products, contact with infected animal tissues, placentas, aborted fetuses, inhalation (laboratory); occupational disease of farmers, veterinarians, butchers, shepherds. Pathogenesis: bacteria survive and multiply in macrophages and spread to reticuloendothelial system; granulomas in liver, spleen, marrow, lymph nodes. Clinical: acute or insidious fever, undulant pattern, drenching sweats, malaise, arthralgia, back pain (spondylitis, sacroiliitis), hepatosplenomegaly, orchitis/epididymitis, endocarditis (main cause of death), neurobrucellosis; chronic brucellosis. Laboratory diagnosis: blood culture (Castaneda, automated systems — prolonged incubation), bone marrow culture (more sensitive); serology — standard tube agglutination test (titre ≥ 1:160; prozone common; blocking antibodies detected by Coombs antiglobulin test; 2-mercaptoethanol test distinguishes IgG — active), Rose Bengal test, ELISA; PCR. Treatment: doxycycline + rifampicin or streptomycin for 6 weeks. Prevention: pasteurisation, animal vaccination.",
[["Species",[["Species","Host","CO2 need","H2S"],["B. melitensis","Goats, sheep","No","No"],["B. abortus","Cattle","Yes","Yes"],["B. suis","Pigs","No","Yes (variable)"],["B. canis","Dogs","No","No"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Blood culture (Castaneda biphasic), prolonged incubation","Bone marrow culture","Standard agglutination test ≥ 1:160","Coombs test for incomplete antibodies; 2-ME test for IgG","ELISA, PCR"]]],
{c:"Brucellosis is an underdiagnosed occupational zoonosis; prolonged cultures and serology with attention to prozone and blocking antibodies are needed for diagnosis."});

Z("micro4",13,"m",["Klebsiella and Proteus are Gram-negative bacilli of the gut: Klebsiella is capsulated and causes pneumonia and hospital infections; Proteus swarms on agar and causes urinary infections with stones.","Klebsiella wears a thick slimy coat (capsule) — mucoid colonies; Proteus runs everywhere, swarming in waves across the plate.",["Klebsiella pneumoniae: non-motile, capsulated, lactose fermenter (mucoid pink colonies), string test; Friedlander's pneumonia (red currant jelly sputum), UTI, neonatal sepsis; ESBL/carbapenemase producer.","Proteus mirabilis/vulgaris: motile, swarming, non-lactose fermenter, urease +, H2S +, fishy odour.","Proteus urease → alkaline urine → struvite (staghorn) stones.","Proteus OX19, OX2, OXK antigens used in Weil–Felix test."],"Klebsiella = 'Capsule, Currant jelly, Carbapenemase'; Proteus = 'Swarming, Stones, Smell'."],
"Klebsiella pneumoniae is a Gram-negative, non-motile, capsulated bacillus of Enterobacteriaceae. On MacConkey agar it forms large, mucoid, pink (lactose-fermenting) colonies; mucoid colonies stretch into a string (string test, hypervirulent strains). Biochemistry: IMViC −−++ (indole −, MR −, VP +, citrate +), urease +. Capsule (K antigens) is the major virulence factor. Diseases: lobar pneumonia (Friedlander pneumonia) in alcoholics and diabetics — necrotising, cavitating, upper lobe, thick 'red currant jelly' sputum; hospital-acquired pneumonia, UTI, septicaemia, neonatal sepsis, liver abscess (hypervirulent strains), wound infections; K. rhinoscleromatis (rhinoscleroma) and K. ozaenae (atrophic rhinitis). It is a major multidrug-resistant pathogen producing ESBLs and carbapenemases (KPC, NDM, OXA-48). Proteus (P. mirabilis, P. vulgaris) are Gram-negative, actively motile, pleomorphic bacilli that swarm in successive waves on blood agar (inhibited by MacConkey, CLED, increased agar or chloral hydrate); non-lactose fermenters with characteristic fishy/burnt-chocolate odour; urease +, H2S +, phenylalanine deaminase +; P. vulgaris is indole +, P. mirabilis indole −. Diseases: UTI (especially catheterised and with structural abnormalities) — urease splits urea to ammonia, alkalinising urine and precipitating struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) stones, including staghorn calculi; wound infections, septicaemia. Proteus OX strains share antigens with rickettsiae (Weil–Felix test).",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Proteus mirabilis"],["Motility","Non-motile","Motile, swarming"],["Capsule","Prominent","Absent"],["Lactose","Fermenter (mucoid pink)","Non-fermenter"],["Urease","+","+++ (rapid)"],["H2S","−","+"],["IMViC","−−++","−+−±"],["Key diseases","Pneumonia, UTI, sepsis, liver abscess","UTI, struvite stones, wound infections"],["Special","ESBL, carbapenemase","Weil–Felix antigens"]]]],
{c:"Klebsiella's capsule and drug resistance and Proteus's urease and swarming explain their clinical patterns and laboratory identification."});

Z("micro4",14,"m",["Yersinia pestis is a Gram-negative coccobacillus causing plague, a flea-borne zoonosis of rats that can spread to humans as bubonic, septicaemic or pneumonic plague.","Rats are the reservoir, fleas the syringe; the bacteria block the flea's gut so it bites repeatedly, injecting bacteria.",["Short, ovoid Gram-negative bacilli with bipolar 'safety-pin' staining (Wayson, methylene blue); non-motile.","Culture: 'stalactite' growth in ghee broth; 'fried egg' colonies on blood agar.","Virulence: F1 capsule, V and W antigens, Yops (type III secretion), plasminogen activator, pH6 antigen.","Forms: bubonic (painful buboes), septicaemic, pneumonic (person to person by droplets, highly fatal). Treatment: streptomycin, gentamicin, doxycycline."],"Plague = 'Safety-pin bacilli, Stalactite growth, Rats & fleas'."],
"Yersinia pestis is a short, plump, Gram-negative coccobacillus showing bipolar (safety-pin) staining with Wayson, Giemsa or methylene blue stains; non-motile, non-sporing, with a capsule (F1 antigen) at 37 °C. It grows slowly on ordinary media; in ghee broth (broth with oil drops) it produces characteristic stalactite growth hanging from the surface; on blood agar small dark-brown colonies; optimum 27 °C. Virulence factors: F1 capsular antigen (antiphagocytic), V and W antigens, Yersinia outer proteins (Yops) delivered by type III secretion (inhibit phagocytosis and cytokines), plasminogen activator (Pla — spread), pH6 antigen, LPS, iron acquisition. Epidemiology: zoonosis of wild rodents (sylvatic plague) and domestic rats (urban plague; Rattus rattus); vector — rat flea Xenopsylla cheopis, in which bacteria block the proventriculus causing repeated feeding and regurgitation of bacilli; India had an outbreak in Surat (1994). Clinical forms: bubonic (commonest; fever, painful enlarged lymph nodes — buboes — in groin, axilla), septicaemic (DIC, gangrene of extremities — 'Black Death'), pneumonic (primary from droplets — person-to-person, rapidly fatal; secondary from septicaemia). Laboratory diagnosis (BSL-3): bubo aspirate, blood, sputum; Gram, Wayson stain (bipolar bacilli); culture; F1 antigen detection (dipstick, ELISA), PCR, serology (passive haemagglutination), phage lysis. Treatment: streptomycin, gentamicin, doxycycline, ciprofloxacin. Prevention: rodent and flea control, chemoprophylaxis of contacts (doxycycline), formalin-killed vaccine (limited use).",
[["Clinical forms",[["Form","Route","Features"],["Bubonic","Flea bite","Fever, painful buboes"],["Septicaemic","Blood spread","Shock, DIC, gangrene"],["Pneumonic","Droplets / secondary","Severe pneumonia, person-to-person, high mortality"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Specimens: bubo aspirate, blood, sputum (BSL-3)","Wayson/methylene blue: bipolar safety-pin bacilli","Culture: ghee broth stalactite, blood agar","F1 antigen RDT, PCR, serology"]]],
{c:"Plague remains a re-emerging zoonosis and potential bioweapon; rapid diagnosis by bipolar staining, antigen detection and early aminoglycoside or doxycycline therapy save lives."});
